  "content": "Owen Carter's scoring has improved, but his approach play is still costing him strokes. Over his last ten rounds (June 12 to September 26) he averaged 76.0. His last two rounds were qualifier rounds at the home course, a 74 and a 71. His average to par is +4.43 over 14 rounds in the stats cache, which runs a little wider than the ten-round window.\n\nHis scores ran from 73 to 82 in July. The 82 came at Poplar Grove, and he followed it with a 73 and a 74.\n\nStrokes gained is per 18 holes over 8 of those rounds, against a Tour baseline of 0:\n- **Total:** -6.74. The team average is -5.79, which puts him 5th of 7. There is no college figure for strokes gained.\n- **Approach:** -4.16, his biggest loss. The team average is -3.09, which puts him 6th of 7.\n- **Putting:** -2.06. The team average is -3.03, which puts him 2nd of 7. Putting is relatively strong for this group.\n- **Off the tee:** -0.32. The team average is +0.41, which puts him 6th of 7.\n- **Around the green:** -0.20. The team average is -0.09, which puts him 4th of 7.\n\nHis greens in regulation is 61.1% over 144 holes. That is above the Division I average of 60%, below the team average of 62.8% (5th of 7), and below the Tour's 66%.\n\nApproach is where practice time would pay off most, with tee play second. Two of the ten rounds have no strokes gained data, so those figures rest on 8 rounds. His most recent round is September 26, so there is nothing newer than that.",
